@startuml
' Phase 10a — Terminal: planned infrastructure.
' Copy to summary.uml at part end and edit to the as-built state.

title Phase 10a — Terminal (planned)

skinparam componentStyle rectangle

package "aa-console (Go; imports contracts only)" as console {
  component "client (control-plane HTTP/JSON + deterministic fake)" as client
  component "command (typed commands + idempotency + audit)" as command
  component "view (read models / browsing)" as view
  component "approval (explicit gate decisions)" as approval
  component "config (workflow + tool/plugin management)" as config
  component "tui (deterministic text surface)" as tui
  component "app (surface wiring + entry point)" as app
}

package "part 10b — surface (extension point)" as surfacepkg {
  component "Surface interface + shared view-model layer" as surface
}

package "aa-contracts (v1 schemas + openapi control plane)" as contracts
component "control plane (phase 10 core: loopback HTTP/JSON, bearer)" as server

contracts --> view
client --> view
client --> command
client --> approval
client --> config
client --> tui
view --> tui
command --> tui
tui --> app
client --> app
app --> surface : generalizes in 10b
client ..> server : HTTP/JSON (loopback, bearer)

note right of client
  ADR-P10A-002: the control-plane
  client is a seam; a deterministic
  fake stands in for tests and offline
  use, and an httptest server exercises
  the OpenAPI paths against phase 10.
end note

note bottom of command
  ADR-P10A-004: every write carries an
  idempotency key derived from the
  command contract id and is audited.
  Repeated commands are replayed safely.
end note

note bottom of view
  ADR-P10A-003: views are pure,
  deterministic projections of client
  responses, ordered by id/sequence,
  never the wall clock, so renders are
  golden-testable.
end note

note bottom of approval
  ADR-P10A-005: approvals and gates
  state their consequences and require
  an explicit decision; nothing is
  auto-resolved (D18).
end note

note bottom of tui
  ADR-P10A-001/007: the console depends
  only on contracts and the control
  plane; the text surface renders with
  stable, golden-tested output. The
  surface abstraction is generalized in
  part 10b, and future surfaces
  (PR #17) implement it.
end note

@enduml
